Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
✔️ Valid
✔️ Common
✔️ Correct

But… not always the best choice.
Now let’s understand why, not just that it works.

🧠 WHY this works (core Java concept)
Queue is an interface
public interface Queue<E> extends Collection<E>
LinkedList implements Queue
public class LinkedList<E>
    implements List<E>, Deque<E>
So this is classic polymorphism:

Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
➡️ You program to the interface, not the implementation
➡️ This is good design

🔍 What operations are you allowed to use?
Because the reference type is Queue:

q.add(10);
q.offer(20);
q.poll();
q.peek();
❌ You cannot do:

q.get(0);   // not allowed
Even though LinkedList supports it — because Queue reference restricts access.

🧠 This is intentional design, not a limitation.

🧱 Now the IMPORTANT part (interview-level clarity)
Should you use LinkedList for Queue?
❌ Usually NO
✅ Prefer ArrayDeque
🔥 Why ArrayDeque is BETTER than LinkedList
Aspect	            LinkedList	        ArrayDeque
Internal structure	Nodes + pointers	Resizable array
Memory usage	    High (prev + next)	Low
Cache locality	    Poor	            Good
Performance	        Slower	            Faster
Null allowed	    Yes	                ❌ No

Recommended way
Queue<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
🧠 This is what interviewers want to hear.

❗ Why LinkedList is still allowed
Because:

It was the original Queue implementation

Needed for backward compatibility

Useful when you also need List operations (rare)

🔑 Interview-ready explanation (MEMORIZE)
“Yes, we can assign a Queue reference to a LinkedList object because LinkedList implements Queue.
However, in practice, ArrayDeque is preferred for queue operations due to better performance and lower memory overhead.”

That sentence alone shows senior-level clarity.

🧩 Deque (important connection)
Deque = Double-ended Queue
Supports:

FIFO (queue)

LIFO (stack)

Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();
dq.addLast(1);   // queue add
dq.removeFirst(); // queue remove
dq.push(2);      // stack push
dq.pop();        // stack pop
🧠 This is why:

Stack → ❌ legacy

Deque → ✅ modern replacement

